{
  "case_id": "2009-09-448",
  "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. ATES: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The Fourth Circuit vacated an award of attorney's fees to Petitioner Hardt even though the district court found Respondent violated ERISA in bad faith and required Respondent to redetermine benefits within 30 days or face adverse judgment. And Ms. Hardt then secured the full disability benefits after that court-enforceable order. Ms. Hardt is entitled to -- is eligible for a fee award under section 502(g)(1) of ERISA by proper application of this Court's established fee standards under any test this Court has previously established."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: How could somebody have some success on the merits if they don't achieve a judgment of some sort?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: -- and you\u2019re essentially saying that when there is simply a procedural victory, which happens all the time, when -- when an agency is -- is reversed in its procedure even though the -- the -- the petitioner here doesn't get any concrete relief until it goes back to the agency and may lose in the agency ultimately, you\u2019re -- you\u2019re content to say that fees are assessable in that situation, just by reason of the procedural victory."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Take the case, though -- just take the -- what is the government's position? The ERISA plaintiff wants $5 million. They get denied everything. The -- the court says: I noticed here there was a 30-day deadline that you had, and he only gave you 28 days, so I'm sending it back, but I'll tell you your claim that there was enough evidence is absurd; you\u2019re never going to win it. And then he goes back, and he loses it. Okay? He has had a procedural victory. Does he get attorney's fees? Not -- not a chance that he\u2019s going to win this claim, and, indeed, he loses it. He doesn't get a penny. Does he get attorney's fees, because on a technicality he won a new hearing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What if -- what if the success is preliminary? You know, the plaintiff survives a motion to dismiss, the plaintiff survives a motion for summary judgment, wins every procedural issue, wins a privilege issue, gets discovery issues resolved, and at the end of the day loses?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: But then, aren't you treating the statute as though it did have a prevailing party clause in it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: That may be, but why can't they do this? What would be wrong with this heretical idea, that as long as the plaintiff wins something out of the court, the district judge -- that group of people we were talking about -- has discretion to decide whether ultimately they got something significant of what they wanted, and as long as that judgment helped them get them something significantly of what they wanted, attorney's fees are fine; and we leave it all up to the district judge as long as the district judge doesn't abuse the discretion that that standard gives him?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Rosenkranz, suppose the complaint was: I asked for a turnover of certain documents; they refused without reason. And the court says: You\u2019re right; you\u2019re entitled to those documents. It's interlocutory; there is no decision; but the only thing that the plaintiff asked for the plaintiff got, that is entitlement to the documents. The court said: You are entitled to the documents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What if --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-09-448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dcda670d3683c788004cd1fae808688d561770bcfbacb55f1d32322a6c979e53",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dcda670d3683-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So what is the difference between prevailing party and some success on the merits for you? The only difference is whether they won on one cause of action as opposed to four?"
    },
